# -*- mode:org;coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-and-interactor-templates — variable windows backed by user-chosen interactors
#+STATUS: DRAFT
#+TYPE: enhancement
#+DEPENDS: tui-module-specification.org (§11), interactor-design-revision.issue

DRAFT — needs pjb's specification. This issue frames the generalisation of
the debugger window layout from the fixed four aldo panes to a variable
number of windows, each backed by a user-chosen /interactor/. It lists the
model, the commands, and the open questions; the answers become the spec.

* Motivation

Today the ncurses debugger has exactly four windows with fixed roles
#+begin_example
(=autolisp-debug-ui-ncurses/source/ncurses-ui.lisp=):

: +window-base-interactor+ = ((:stack . :framenav) (:source . :navi)
:                             (:interactor . :aldo) (:repl . :repl))
#+end_example

=C-w 2= / =C-w 3= (=window-split=) do not really /add/ a window: to keep the
four-pane aldo layout they split one pane and, in effect, drop another —
the window count is pinned at four and each window's content is fixed by its
=window-role=. There is no way for the user to open a fifth window, to close
one, or to say /what runs/ in a given window.

We want the general case: a *variable number of windows*, and *commands so the
user chooses what runs in each window*.

* The model: windows backed by interactors

The Emacs analogy is deliberate but partial. In Emacs a window displays a
/buffer/; a buffer is a text container, and /some/ buffers are attached to a
process (shell, REPL, comint). Here we have no free-standing text buffers:
the only things that back a window are *interactors* (aldo, navi, sedit, the
Lisp REPL, the inspector, the stack browser, …). A file or a data structure
is not shown as a passive buffer but /through/ an interactor that reads,
evaluates and prints it (navi/sedit for structure, the inspector for objects).

So the mapping is:

: Emacs window  -> displays a buffer          | our window -> runs an interactor
: Emacs buffer  -> text, maybe a process       | our backing -> an interactor activation
: (no analogue) -> pick major-mode / buffer     | pick an *interactor template* to instantiate

An *interactor template* is an interactor /kind/ that can be instantiated in a
window (aldo, navi, sedit, lisp-repl, inspector, stack-browser, …). Creating
a window means choosing a template and instantiating it; the UI should
*present the list of available templates* for the user to choose from.

* Core design tension: singletons vs. per-window activations

In a given lisp image, some interactor are essentially singletons,
while other interactor may be instanciated several times.  Namely, the
lisp repl interactor, and the aldo debugger interactor are coupled,
and each a singleton in a lisp image, while the other interactors can
be instanciated multiple times.

Now this could be refined.

A lisp repl is composed of several layers: a  user interface: the
result printing function, the prompt printing function, the lisp
expression reader (multiplexed with the command reader and
dispatcher), and the actual lisp evaluator.

This is only this lisp evaluator component that need to be a singleton.

So, we could have several lisp interactors, all of them using the same
lisp evaluator, and multiplexing their access to the lisp
evaluator. However the problem would be that the state of the lisp
image, which the lisp evaluator gives access to, would be the same for
the different lisp interactors, but they would have only a partial
view of this state, since they would get only the results of the
expressions each of them sent to the evaluator.

#+begin_example
interactor 1           evaluator(state)         interactor 2
      |                      |                       |
(setq x 42)  ----eval------> x=42                    |
      42    <----result------+                       |
      |                    x=33 <-------eval----- (setq x 33)
      |                     +--------result-------> 33
      x      ----eval------->                        |
      33    <----result ----+                        |
#+end_example

This is what we have with slime-mrepl, where we have multiple slime
repl interfacing with the same *inferior-lisp* evaluator.

Similarly for the aldo debugger.  The debugger contains state, and
need to be a singleton in the lisp image, since it works with the only
lisp evaluator thread there is.  But we could split the interactor
side from it, and allow multiple aldo interactor using the same aldo
debugger.

Since it is the user who choose to instanciate a new interactor, and
having documented this particularity, I move to allow it, just like
slime-mrepl is an option available for Common Lisp programmers in
slime.

This requires some refactoring of the lisp interactor and the aldo
interactor.

Once this refactoring is done, all interactor can be instanciated
multiple times, and the presence of singletons the lisp evaluator and
the aldo debugger is hidden and implicit. 

Note: the lisp evaluator works in a lisp image which belongs either
to the CAD application, or to a specific drawing (DWG) document. Thus
each lisp evaluator can be qualified by the name of the cad
application ("cadtui"), or the uniquified name of the document
(eg. "dessin 1#2", if it's the second document named "dessin 1"). This
name can and will be used to disambiguate lisp evaluators once we have
multiple document support. Since each aldo debugger is associated to a
single lisp evaluator, it inherits this document name ("debugging
dessin 1#2").

When we have multiple interactors using the same background singleton,
their name need to be dismbiguated too:

- "lisp repl dessin 1": interactor to the evaluator of the document
  named dessin 1; 

- "lisp repl dessin 1#2": interactor to the evaluator of the 2nd
  document named dessin 1;

- "lisp repl dessin 1#2<2>": second interactor to the evaluator of the 2nd
  document named dessin 1.


Therefore this is not true anymore:

#+begin_quote
The interactor framework (=autolisp-interactor/source/interactor.lisp=) makes
each interactor a *singleton* (one =*sedit*=, one =*navi*=, …) with a single
global =*interactor-stack*=; per-entry data lives in the /activation/ (an
=interactor= + a =state=).
#+end_quote

Now we have interactor classes (or interactor templates, not
necessarily real CLOS classes):
- lisp repl
- aldo debugger
- navi source navigator
- sedit sexp/source editor      
- inspect sexp/data inspector
- stack inspector

These interactor classes can be instanciated.
These interactor instances live in a stack of interactors, and
interface with window, which may be displayed in a frame.
(with variable number of windows, windows may be visible or hidden).

And here we would need a new class of interactor, to show and select
an item in a list, eg. show the list of windows, and select one to
become visible in place of the list of item window.


A window/interactor world needs:

  1. *Multiple concurrent activations of the same template* — e.g. two navi
     windows on two different files, or two inspectors on two objects. Each
     window then owns its own interactor stack (the =windows.lisp= window
     already carries a =stack= slot; =ncurses-ui.lisp= notes "Full aldo /
     sedit / navi line commands need the per-window interactor stacks").

  2. A rule for the *global* =*interactor-stack*= vs. the *per-window* stacks:
     which stack is active is presumably the /selected window/'s stack, and
     key dispatch / command routing runs against it. This has to be pinned
     down precisely (see Questions).

  3. *Unify the two "interactor" notions.* Today they are NOT the same object.
     The §5 framework's =*interactor-stack*= holds =activation= structs
     wrapping real interactor singletons (=*sedit*=, =*navi*= …). But the
     ncurses per-window =window-stack= (=windows.lisp= =stack= slot, seeded in
     =ncurses-ui.lisp= from =+window-base-interactor+=) holds *bare keywords*
     (=:navi=, =:aldo=, =:framenav=, =:repl=, =:window-manager=) — a parallel,
     ad-hoc stack unrelated to the framework's activations. The generalisation
     must make a window's stack a stack of *real activations*, so that
     selecting a window selects its interactor stack for real command routing
     and config-cascade resolution.

This is the crux. Everything else (commands, layout, config) follows once the
"a window owns an interactor stack; a template can be instantiated N times"
model is fixed.


Points 2 and 3 need to be rethought:

A- we don't need a global interactor stack since we always have at
least one window in one frame: the default lisp repl window in the
initial tty-frame. This initial tty-frame is created with an
interactor stack that contains only the lisp interactor of the
application, initially.  If we enter the debugger or call another
interactor without creating a new window, these interactor will be
stacked above the lisp repl in this default window of the initial
tty-frame. We get the original behavior.

B- Indeed, the active interactor stack is the interactor stack of the
selected window.

C- One thing to consider however when we introduce multiple window.
Let's start from [(lisp)/default-window/tty-frame].

Say we enter the ncursor debugger: we created a vdt-frame, with 4
windows:
- [(stack aldo lisp)/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(inspect aldo lisp)/inspect-window/vdt-frame]
- [(navi aldo lisp)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [(aldo lisp)/debug-window/vdt-frame]
plus the original window/frame (both hidden):
- [(lisp)/default-window/tty-frame]

Note how each window has an interactor stack. We must precise the
structure sharing in these stacks:

- [(stack . #1=(aldo lisp<2>))/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(inspect . #1#)/inspect-window/vdt-frame]
- [(navi . #1#)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [#1#/debug-window/vdt-frame]

Since the tty-frame and its default window are hidden behind the
vdt-frame, we momentarily cannot use the lisp interactor of this
default window (it would display results in the default window which
is hidden). Therefore the debugger must instanciate a new lisp
interactor (sharing the same lisp evaluator), hence "lisp<2>". This
lisp interactor, along with the aldo interactor use the debug-window
for I/O. This stack (aldo lisp<2>) is shared and used in the 3 other
stacks. This means that debugging commands and lisp evaluation
commands are processed buy the same aldo and lisp evaluators, and
results are displayed in the debug-window (which is what we want).
The navi interactor displays its navigation results in its own
source-window, the inspect interactor displays its inspection results
in its own inspect-window, and the stack interactor displays its stack
frames in its own stack-window.
The command that swaps sedit for navi woud pop the navi interactor
from the source-window interactor stack and push a new sedit
interactor.

- [(stack . #1=(aldo lisp<2>))/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(inspect . #1#)/inspect-window/vdt-frame]
- [(sedit . #1#)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [#1#/debug-window/vdt-frame]

If the user creates a new window in this vdt-frame, for example, a new
sedit, it would give the command from any of the existing window,
which has a lisp evaluator at the bottom of its stack, that can be
reused for the bottom of the new sedit stack:

- [(sedit<2> . #1)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [(stack . #1=(aldo lisp<2>))/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(inspect . #1#)/inspect-window/vdt-frame]
- [(sedit . #1#)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [#1#/debug-window/vdt-frame]

Similarly, the user may create a new stack frame window, which allows
him to view at the same time two different frames in the backtraces:

- [(sedit<2> . #1)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [(stack<2> . #1=(aldo lisp<2>))/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(stack . #1=(aldo lisp<2>))/stack-window/vdt-frame]
- [(inspect . #1#)/inspect-window/vdt-frame]
- [(sedit . #1#)/source-window/vdt-frame]
- [#1#/debug-window/vdt-frame]

D- so yes, the generalisation must make a window's stack a stack of
*real activations*, so that selecting a window selects its interactor
stack for real command routing and config-cascade resolution.

* Interactor templates (registry)

Introduce a registry of interactor templates. Candidate initial set:

  - =lisp-repl=    the Lisp/AutoLISP read-eval-print
  - =aldo=         the debugger command interactor
  - =navi=         structure navigator (poll-point motions)
  - =sedit=        structure editor (all-subsexp motions, editing)
  - =inspector=    object inspector (=clal-inspect=)
  - =stack-browser= the SLDB-style backtrace browser (§8)
  - (later) a directory/=dired=-like template, a help/apropos template

Each template needs:
- a display name,
- a one-line description (shown in the picker),
- one or more (contextual) constructors that instantiates an activation over a given target
  (a form / object / file / stack),
- the config it runs under (its =<name>.conf= cascade).

The constructor context includes
- the window/frame and window interactor stack,
- the target object,
- a save or quit continuation (eg. the clal-sedit function must return
  the edited sexp that can be stored, or evaluated, etc. it must
  therefore be able to collect this edited sexp once the q command is
  given to the interactor it creates; similarly for an intermediate
  save: (clal-sedit 'my-function) doesn't save to a file, but the save
  command could redefine my-function with the new definition, thru a
  save continuation).

All templates are *multi-instance* but some templates may refer/use
singletons internally:
- lisp-repl use a singleton lisp evaluator
- aldo uses a singleton debugger.  

  
* Commands (window management)

Extend the =C-w= / =C-x= window-manager interactor
(=run-window-command=, =ncurses-ui.lisp=):

  - *create a window* choosing a template: split the selected window (or add
    to the layout) and prompt with the template list — e.g. =C-w c= opens the
    picker; or =C-w 2= / =C-w 3= keep splitting but the /new/ window then
    prompts for its template instead of inheriting a fixed role.
  - *set what runs in the selected window*: replace its backing interactor
    with a chosen template (=C-w RET= / =C-w m=), like =switch-to-buffer=.
  - *close* the selected window (=C-w 0=), *close others* (=C-w 1=) — with
    the window count now free.
  - *list templates* (the picker itself), reachable on demand.
  - the existing motions (=C-w n/p/o/=, swap, resize, balance) carry over.

The fixed four-pane aldo layout becomes just the *default layout* the
debugger opens with (backward compatible), expressed as a saved layout (see
config), not a hard constraint.

* Relationship to the config cascade (parallel open work)

Per =tui-module-specification.org= §11 and the config work already landed
(=tui-core= =config.lisp=): each interactor names the config it runs under,
and the interactor stack's configs form the cascade (faces, key bindings,
print-syntax, layout). Windows and interactor templates plug straight in: a
window's selected template sets =*active-config*= to that template's config,
and its cascade parallels the window's interactor stack. Two questions this
raises are listed below; they should be answered together with the window
model since they share the "interactor backs a window" structure.

* Open questions (for pjb to specify)

  1. *Per-window interactor stacks.* Confirm the model: each window owns an
     interactor stack; the selected window's stack is the active one; the
     global =*interactor-stack*= becomes (or is replaced by) the selected
     window's stack.

  PJB: yes, indeed.

     How do the four debugger panes — today one logical aldo
     session across four views — map onto per-window stacks? Is aldo one
     activation shared by the stack/source/inspect panes, with each pane a
     /view/ onto it, while only the =:interactor= pane owns the aldo command
     stack? Or is each pane its own activation?

  PJB: stacks can be shared structure in other stacks.
       So the stack of aldo and lisp<2> interactor of the debugger window
       is shared as bottom of stacks for the other windows, and thus these
       interactors are accessible in the command dispatching.

  2. *Multi-instance templates.* Which templates may have several live
     instances (navi, sedit, inspector — surely yes; aldo — surely one per
     debugged thread)? The singleton =define-interactor= objects stay as the
     /template/; a window holds an /activation/ of it. Confirm.

  PJB: Yes, all interactor are multiple instance. Some refer/use an
       internal singleton.

  3. *The template picker.* What triggers it (=C-w c=? a menu?), how is the
     list rendered (a transient window? a minibuffer completion?), and what
     is the template metadata shown (name + description)?

 PJB: I would rather see a set of commands:
|                                                |     |                                                                               |
| Command                                        | Key | Description                                                                   |
|------------------------------------------------+-----+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| M-x make-lisp-window RET                       |     | creates a new repl in the same lisp image (same lisp evaluator)               |
| M-x make-aldo-window RET                       |     | creates a new aldo window in the same lisp image aldo<2> same debugger        |
| M-x make-navi-window RET <source-ref> RET      |     | create a new navi window with the same context as current window              |
| M-x make-sedit-window RET <object> RET         |     | create a new sedit window with the same context as the current window         |
| M-x make-inspector-window RET <object> RET     |     | create a new inspector window with the same context as the current window     |
| M-x make-stack-browser-window RET <object> RET |     | create a new stack-browser window with the same context as the current window |

 

  4. *Instantiation targets.* When the user picks e.g. =navi= or =inspector=
     for a new window, where does its target come from — the current
     selection, a prompt, the value in the REPL, the object under point?

  PJB: Yes.
      Yes, indeed, we take context from the current window, including possibly selection (point),
      but if not available, it's also possible to read input from the minibuffer.
  
  5. *Layout persistence.* Layouts saved as a =:layout= setting in a config
     (which one — =aldo.conf= for the debugger's default? a top-level
     =layout.conf=?). Restore on entry; the fixed 4-pane layout becomes the
     debugger's default saved layout.
     
 PJB: So, for the debugger fixed 4-windows, it's easy: we know what
      interactor to put in the windows and how to instanciate them.
      What about user-defined interactor. I guess we can record their
      constructor parameters and replay them.  So the user can define
      a frame with a lisp REPL, an inspector on some data, and 2 sedit
      on different files, and restoring the layout would recreate the
      interactors too. In the case of the debugger, the layout would
      be instanciated dynamically when we enter the debugger, with a
      new debugging context. Other layout would be called up by the
      user (M-x load-layout RET my-programming-layout RET)
      
      
 
  6. *Config cascade when stacked.* =clal-sedit= from the REPL runs
     =sedit.conf -> lisp.conf=; sedit entered above a debugger stop runs
     (per the stack) =sedit.conf -> aldo.conf -> lisp.conf=. The static
     config parent (=ensure-config "sedit" "lisp"=) gives only the first.
     Should the cascade be computed *dynamically from the live interactor
     stack* rather than from the static parent chain, so a template's cascade
     depends on where it is stacked? (Recommended, but confirm.)

  PJB: yes absolutely.

  7. *Config directory / save timing.* The /where/ is already solved: use the
     existing XDG resolver in =autolisp-debug-ui/source/settings.lisp= —
     =config-save-path= (=$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clautolisp/NAME.conf=) and
     =config-load-path= (save-path if present, else along =$XDG_CONFIG_DIRS=).
     Open part: *when* to save each =<name>.conf= — on interactor exit, on
     every change (the config =dirty= flag exists), or only on an explicit
     save command? And do we save one file per config in the cascade, or only
     the innermost/changed ones?

  PJB: we want to use an explicit configuration saving, because some
       setting may be broken/unusable, so saving automatically is not
       recommended.

  8. *tty frames.* In a tty-frame (no ncurses) the "windows" collapse; the
     variable-window commands are ncurses-only, but the interactor templates
     and their config cascades must still work for =clal-sedit= /
     =clal-inspect= from a plain REPL (they draw line-oriented, window ops are
     no-ops). Confirm the tty degradation is acceptable as-is.

  PJB: Indeed. We still have the frame and window object, but we need
  not manage multiple window in the tty-frame. The interactor stacking
  is enough.

* Status of the pieces (2026-08, branch debugger-ui-ncurses-aldb)

  - sedit is *already* a full interactor (=*sedit*=, =sedit-enter= over
    =interactor-loop=); =clal-sedit= and the debugger bridge drive it. What is
    missing is (a) the config-cascade binding (Q6/Q7) and (b) windowed
    rendering (it is line-oriented today).
  - The config cascade, per-config faces and key bindings, window ops
    (=clear-window= / =move-cursor-to= / =window-put=), opaque object handles,
    and the §13 print-syntax modules are landed in =tui-core= / the runtime.
  - The window struct already has a =stack= slot awaiting the per-window
    interactor stacks; =window-split= still enforces the four-pane layout (it
    re-homes an existing window rather than adding/removing one).
  - The tool's integration point is =resolve-default-debugger-ui=
    (=tools/clautolisp/source/main.lisp:302=), where lower systems already get
    their upper-layer hooks installed (=*default-on-quit-policy*=,
    =*aldo-configuration-writer*=, shell-escape). Since =autolisp-sedit= cannot
    see =tui-core=, any config-cascade binding for sedit is installed here as a
    hook (the =*default-on-quit-policy*= precedent).
  - =print-syntax.lisp= (§13) defines print modules for stack / inspect / aldo
    / repl but *not* sedit; a =with-sedit-print-syntax= would be added if
    sedit's windowed rendering needs one.

Once Q1–Q8 are answered, the implementation is: a template registry + picker,
per-window interactor stacks, the =C-w= create/set/close commands, dynamic
config cascade from the live stack, and layout persistence.
